1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,236
Listen to one sentence,
inside a simulated cathedral.

2
00:00:04,225 --> 00:00:07,375
Every room you play in
is asking you a question.

3
00:00:10,433 --> 00:00:13,517
Hear that? Every word is still
ringing when the next one arrives.

4
00:00:13,557 --> 00:00:15,322
Same sentence, same cathedral.

5
00:00:15,504 --> 00:00:18,386
This time, slower, with
room between the phrases.

6
00:00:18,991 --> 00:00:25,031
Every room you play in
is asking you a question.

7
00:00:28,991 --> 00:00:31,826
Nobody touched the cathedral.
The speaker changed.

8
00:00:31,866 --> 00:00:34,572
Hold on to that, because
it's the whole video.

9
00:00:34,874 --> 00:00:37,036
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

10
00:00:37,056 --> 00:00:39,509
This video is about a
book: How Music Works,

11
00:00:39,529 --> 00:00:42,497
by David Byrne, the
singer from Talking Heads.

12
00:00:42,633 --> 00:00:45,081
Its big idea is that music is
shaped by the room it's played in.

13
00:00:45,121 --> 00:00:47,816
I wanted to know how that
shaping actually happens,

14
00:00:47,836 --> 00:00:51,252
so I went looking for people who
measured it. Here's what I found.

15
00:00:51,292 --> 00:00:53,693
The room doesn't write the
music. It asks a question.

16
00:00:53,733 --> 00:00:55,557
A player hears it, and answers.

17
00:00:55,816 --> 00:00:59,404
And different players answer
the same room in different ways.

18
00:00:59,674 --> 00:01:02,522
In the book's first chapter,
Byrne argues that music gets made,

19
00:01:02,542 --> 00:01:05,128
knowingly or not, to fit
the places it'll be heard.

20
00:01:05,168 --> 00:01:07,999
On the radio he gave the
clearest example I've found.

21
00:01:08,039 --> 00:01:11,154
Bach's church was smaller than
the Gothic cathedrals before it,

22
00:01:11,174 --> 00:01:13,019
so Bach could write fast, busy lines.

23
00:01:13,059 --> 00:01:16,321
All of that, Byrne said, would just
turn into mush in a Gothic cathedral.

24
00:01:16,361 --> 00:01:18,754
And the chant that came
before barely changes key.

25
00:01:18,794 --> 00:01:20,206
So in a huge stone room,

26
00:01:20,526 --> 00:01:23,585
every note left hanging in
the air is still in tune.

27
00:01:24,266 --> 00:01:26,189
His own band is his best example.

28
00:01:26,229 --> 00:01:27,520
CBGB, in New York, was a
small, long, narrow club.

29
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:31,134
In the book he describes a
room with very little echo,

30
00:01:31,154 --> 00:01:33,996
where every detail of a song came through.

31
00:01:34,408 --> 00:01:36,806
Later, Talking Heads played Carnegie Hall.

32
00:01:36,846 --> 00:01:39,187
And Byrne noticed that some
of the songs he'd written

33
00:01:39,207 --> 00:01:41,972
didn't sound all that
great in halls like that.

34
00:01:42,149 --> 00:01:44,375
That's composers adjusting over centuries.

35
00:01:44,415 --> 00:01:47,874
But if Byrne is right, it should show
up in a single player on a single night.

36
00:01:47,914 --> 00:01:50,208
Musicians have said so for a long time.

37
00:01:50,251 --> 00:01:54,047
In 1752, the flautist Johann
Joachim Quantz told players

38
00:01:54,067 --> 00:01:57,341
to slow down in echoing rooms,
so the notes don't blur together.

39
00:01:57,381 --> 00:02:00,146
Which is exactly what I
did with that sentence.

40
00:02:00,574 --> 00:02:02,256
And then someone actually checked.

41
00:02:02,296 --> 00:02:05,804
Two researchers in Berlin recorded
the cellist Jean-Guihen Queyras

42
00:02:05,824 --> 00:02:09,880
playing all six of Bach's cello suites
in seven European concert halls.

43
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:13,839
They measured how much each hall echoed,
and how fast he played every movement.

44
00:02:13,879 --> 00:02:17,879
In the most echoing halls, he slowed
down. Quantz would have nodded.

45
00:02:18,207 --> 00:02:19,776
Then the part that made me stop.

46
00:02:19,816 --> 00:02:23,505
In the driest halls, the ones with
almost no echo, he slowed down too.

47
00:02:23,545 --> 00:02:26,550
Too much echo, slower.
Too little echo, also slower.

48
00:02:26,590 --> 00:02:28,623
And one measure of the
hall's echo accounted

49
00:02:28,643 --> 00:02:31,152
for about 74% of how his tempo moved.

50
00:02:31,305 --> 00:02:34,834
That's one player in seven rooms.
But it's a strong pattern.

51
00:02:35,090 --> 00:02:36,914
So why slow down in a dry room?

52
00:02:36,956 --> 00:02:39,120
He told them that in
echoing halls he slows down

53
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:42,046
to avoid a muddy sound, and
lets the pauses run longer.

54
00:02:42,086 --> 00:02:45,099
For the dry halls, the explanation
is the researchers' own.

55
00:02:45,139 --> 00:02:47,669
With no echo to carry a note,
a player may hold notes longer,

56
00:02:47,689 --> 00:02:49,501
to make up for sound that isn't there.

57
00:02:49,541 --> 00:02:52,318
That's their reading, not his
words. But look at what it means.

58
00:02:52,358 --> 00:02:55,358
The same answer, slower,
to two opposite questions.

59
00:02:55,890 --> 00:02:57,993
So the same team tried it on more people.

60
00:02:58,033 --> 00:03:00,882
Twelve musicians, fourteen
simulated concert halls.

61
00:03:00,922 --> 00:03:03,984
And here, the hall's echo
explained under 9% of their tempo,

62
00:03:04,004 --> 00:03:06,602
even after allowing for
each player's own habits.

63
00:03:06,642 --> 00:03:09,095
The paper calls their
strategies very individual.

64
00:03:09,135 --> 00:03:11,297
Almost all of them said
they lengthen pauses

65
00:03:11,317 --> 00:03:13,559
in echoing rooms, but
mostly in slow pieces.

66
00:03:13,599 --> 00:03:17,128
In fast pieces, they kept the
notes short and clean instead.

67
00:03:17,482 --> 00:03:20,986
And to be fair to the simpler story,
two earlier studies looked for it.

68
00:03:21,026 --> 00:03:22,663
One used the Cleveland Orchestra,

69
00:03:22,683 --> 00:03:25,623
the other a grand piano in
a room with adjustable echo.

70
00:03:25,663 --> 00:03:29,231
Neither found the simple pattern:
more echo, slower playing. That fits.

71
00:03:29,271 --> 00:03:30,772
The room doesn't set the tempo.

72
00:03:30,812 --> 00:03:34,283
It sets a problem, and each
player solves it their own way.

73
00:03:34,449 --> 00:03:36,708
So here's a way to
listen for this anywhere.

74
00:03:36,748 --> 00:03:38,765
When a place seems to
have shaped some music,

75
00:03:38,805 --> 00:03:40,326
listen for what the room takes away,

76
00:03:40,346 --> 00:03:42,930
who's hearing it, and what
they changed to get it back.

77
00:03:42,970 --> 00:03:47,287
Then the test. If every player answers the
same way, the room is close to deciding.

78
00:03:47,327 --> 00:03:49,974
If they answer differently,
it's only asking.

79
00:03:50,207 --> 00:03:54,651
It isn't only people. In 2003,
researchers in Leiden recorded great tits,

80
00:03:54,671 --> 00:03:57,906
a small songbird, in quiet
and noisy parts of the city.

81
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:00,019
Traffic noise is mostly low rumble.

82
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:02,775
The birds in the noisy spots
sang with a higher lowest note,

83
00:04:02,815 --> 00:04:06,322
lifting their songs above it.
Here the room isn't echo. It's noise.

84
00:04:06,362 --> 00:04:08,656
And the answer isn't tempo. It's pitch.

85
00:04:09,090 --> 00:04:11,767
Byrne's book makes the same
argument about machines.

86
00:04:11,807 --> 00:04:14,407
He starts with Edison's cylinders,
when bands had to perform

87
00:04:14,427 --> 00:04:17,534
the same song again and again
for each batch of recordings.

88
00:04:17,574 --> 00:04:19,740
A recording format is
a room made of minutes.

89
00:04:19,780 --> 00:04:23,309
In 1925, Igor Stravinsky had a
contract to record his music.

90
00:04:23,434 --> 00:04:25,834
So he wrote a piano suite,
his Serenade in A,

91
00:04:25,874 --> 00:04:29,760
with every movement short enough
to fit one side of a 78 rpm record.

92
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:31,506
About three minutes, at most.

93
00:04:31,932 --> 00:04:34,113
The newest room is a
playlist you can skip.

94
00:04:34,153 --> 00:04:36,507
A researcher at Ohio State,
Hubert Léveillé Gauvin,

95
00:04:36,527 --> 00:04:41,276
studied 303 US top-10
hits from 1986 to 2015.

96
00:04:41,705 --> 00:04:43,670
The wait before the voice
comes in fell from more

97
00:04:43,690 --> 00:04:46,027
than twenty seconds in the
mid-1980s to about five.

98
00:04:46,067 --> 00:04:48,215
And talking to a reporter,
he put it like this.

99
00:04:48,255 --> 00:04:51,093
It's just adapting yourself
to your environment,

100
00:04:51,113 --> 00:04:53,113
whether you're aware of it or not.

101
00:04:53,510 --> 00:04:55,692
That's Byrne's argument,
from someone who counted.

102
00:04:55,732 --> 00:04:59,393
One caution. In a second study,
he compared hits with less popular songs

103
00:04:59,413 --> 00:05:02,295
by the same artists, and
the pattern didn't hold.

104
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:03,948
A short intro doesn't make a hit.

105
00:05:03,988 --> 00:05:07,341
So the room shapes the music.
It still doesn't decide it.

106
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:09,576
Before I play mine, try yours.

107
00:05:09,688 --> 00:05:11,781
In a moment, clap once, wherever you are.

108
00:05:11,821 --> 00:05:15,299
If the clap hangs in the air after
your hands stop, your room has echo.

109
00:05:15,339 --> 00:05:17,810
If it dies right away, it's dry. Go ahead.

110
00:05:20,782 --> 00:05:24,572
Now say the sentence, every room you
play in is asking you a question,

111
00:05:24,612 --> 00:05:26,730
at the pace your room seems to want.

112
00:05:30,990 --> 00:05:33,626
Did you speed up, or slow
down? That's your answer.

113
00:05:33,666 --> 00:05:35,894
A real answer names what
your room was doing,

114
00:05:35,934 --> 00:05:38,035
blurring your words or swallowing them.

115
00:05:38,075 --> 00:05:40,664
Just saying it felt
different doesn't count.

116
00:05:40,873 --> 00:05:44,573
Here's mine, in two rooms like the
ones the cellist slowed down in.

117
00:05:44,613 --> 00:05:45,849
First, the cathedral.

118
00:05:46,723 --> 00:05:52,763
Every room you play in
is asking you a question.

119
00:05:56,809 --> 00:05:58,720
And now, almost no echo at all.

120
00:05:59,698 --> 00:06:03,318
Every room you play in
is asking you a question.

121
00:06:04,031 --> 00:06:06,009
Both slower than I'd normally say it.

122
00:06:06,049 --> 00:06:08,018
One is waiting for the echo to clear.

123
00:06:08,058 --> 00:06:11,492
The other is filling in for an
echo that never comes. Same answer.

124
00:06:11,532 --> 00:06:12,650
Opposite questions.

125
00:06:13,498 --> 00:06:14,734
Every room is asking.

126
00:06:14,859 --> 00:06:18,523
Tanmay Kulkarni, in for
Humanitarians AI, signing off.
